### 3.5 Pattern and decisions

ADR-01: Use a layered modular monolith; routers validate HTTP, services enforce policy, repositories query data. Reject microservices for this release because distributed deployment and transactions add unnecessary risk. ADR-02: PostgreSQL provides constraints, locks and pg_trgm for search. ADR-03: JWT plus a database token_version supports immediate all-session logout; every protected request reads the active user. ADR-04: Keep a single-instance in-memory rate limiter for Sprint 1, recording its limits; use a shared store before scaling.


### 3.5.1 Matching algorithm

Normalize ISBN by removing hyphens; normalize textual search by trim/lowercase. Exclude deleted/unavailable listings, inactive owners and the borrower's own listings. For an ISBN request, require exact ISBN. For a text request, require title/author containment or title trigram similarity >=0.3. Score = 70*relevance + 20*same_location + 10*(condition_rank/4). Relevance is 1 for exact ISBN or title trigram similarity in [0,1] for text requests. Sort descending score, then newest listing, then listing ID for stable ties; return at most five. This scoring is a proposed Sprint 2 baseline, not a performance claim.

Suggestions do not reserve inventory. On acceptance, lock the request, listing and match in a consistent order; recheck eligibility and participant identity. Once both accept, create exactly one active exchange, mark listing RESERVED and request FULFILLED in one transaction. A partial unique index on active exchange listing_id and a unique active request constraint prevent concurrent winners. Re-match on decline, timeout or cancellation; retain history and mark obsolete suggestions stale.


